\paragraph{Results.}
Although a substantial gap to human performance remains, the proposed U-Net clearly outperforms the other systems.
The Flat model performs worst, consistent with earlier findings that default velocity is musically inadequate \cite{kuo2021velocity}.
The seen, less-seen, and unseen repertoire groups are best interpreted as descriptive groups rather than as a strict complexity-controlled comparison.
Even so, the relatively narrow 95\% confidence intervals across groups suggest that participants rated the stimuli with reasonable consistency.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:velcomp:discussion}

This chapter has verified that note-level dynamics can be recovered usefully even when the symbolic notes and timing are held fixed. It turns expressive enhancement into a controllable post-processing stage rather than a full performance-rendering problem, which is exactly the setting encountered in many MIDI-production and corpus-curation workflows.

The image-colourisation view is effective because it matches the structure of the data. Polyphonic note patterns create meaningful vertical and local spatial regularities that are awkward to express in purely sequential terms. The U-Net captures this local structure well, and the sparsity-aware loss prevents the model from spending most of its capacity on silent regions. The results suggest that this representation choice is a major reason the method surpasses the sequence-based baselines considered here.

The evaluation design is therefore deliberately broader than a single regression score. Objective error metrics quantify note-wise accuracy, \(SD_{\mathrm{velo}}\) checks whether the predicted velocity distribution retains expressive spread, and the listening test verifies that the recovered layer changes perceived musical quality. This combination is important because a velocity-completion model can be numerically close to the target while still sounding too flat, or can sound more expressive while producing larger point-wise deviations.

The empirical scope of the chapter is piano-based, using datasets recorded with Yamaha Disklavier instruments.
Within that scope, the cross-dataset MAESTRO-to-SMD results show that the image-colourisation formulation is not merely memorising one corpus.


\section{Summary}
\label{sec:velcomp_summary}
This chapter studied the most controlled form of expressive-label recovery: the notes, onsets, and offsets are already fixed, and only the missing MIDI velocity layer is inferred. By casting the piano roll as an image-like representation, the proposed U-Net uses local pitch--time neighbourhoods and vertical chordal patterns that are weakened in a purely sequential formulation. Window attention and an onset-masked, velocity-aware loss are not incidental additions; they match the sparse structure of the target and prevent the model from optimising mainly around silence.

The chapter also shows why velocity completion should not be evaluated only as point-wise regression. The MAESTRO-to-SMD experiments indicate that the model transfers beyond its training corpus, while \(SD_{\mathrm{velo}}\) and the listening test expose the musical problem that MAE alone can hide: a prediction may be close to the target and still sound too flat. This makes the chapter the symbolic-only baseline for the thesis. It preserves the composition exactly, treats dynamics as an editable layer, and establishes that such a layer has perceptual value before any audio evidence is introduced.

